[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe bug:[22m The C program uses [38;5;10mfgets()[0m and [38;5;10mfputs()[0m, which operate on C strings (NUL-terminated). When the input contains a [38;5;10m\0[0m byte, [38;5;10mfgets[0m stores it in the buffer but [38;5;10mfputs[0m treats it as the string terminator — so only the bytes [3mbefore[23m the NUL get written to stdout.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation was reading raw bytes and passing them through without any NUL-awareness. It faithfully echoed the [38;5;10m\0[0m byte and everything after it, producing [38;5;10m"Null Containing\x00 String"[0m instead of the expected [38;5;10m"Null Containing"[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe fix:[22m Added a check for [38;5;10mbyte[0] == 0[0m in the byte-reading loop. When a NUL is encountered, the code writes whatever has been buffered so far and exits — matching the C behavior where [38;5;10mfputs[0m stops at the first NUL terminator.[0m[0m
